.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt IMP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imp
.Nd compile IMP72 programs to native code
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c | S | Sir
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Fl -target Ns = Ns Ar triple
.Op Fl -sysroot Ns = Ns Ar dir
.Op Fl -runtime Ns = Ns Ar file
.Op Fl s Ar letters
.Op Fl I Ar image
.Op Fl C Ar syntax
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl -run
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl i
.Op Fl s Ar letters
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl -version | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
compiles programs in IMP72, the extensible systems language of Ned Irons as
implemented by Walter Bilofsky for the PDP-10 (DECUS 10-198a), to native code,
and links them.
IMP stands for Irons Meta Processor: it is named for Ned Irons, who invented it, as LISP is named for its List Processor.
The syntax of the language is read at start from
.Pa syntax.imc
and the C interface from
.Pa ffi.imc .
The code generator makes an intermediate representation, and LLVM, inside the
compiler's own process, makes the machine code.
Executables are linked with lld, in the process where lld is installed as a
library and otherwise by running
.Xr ld.lld 1
.Pq Xr ld64.lld 1
on macOS.
No other compiler or linker runs.
.Pp
The word of IMP72 is 64 bits and its arguments are passed by reference.
A
.Sy SUBR
is therefore a C function of pointers to its arguments, returning a 64-bit word.
.Sy CCALL F(A, B)
calls the C function
.Fn F
with the values of
.Va A
and
.Va B .
A variable declared
.Sy COMMON
is a global symbol.
.Pp
An argument ending in
.Pa .o ,
.Pa .a
or
.Pa .so
is linked.
A file of syntax and declarations is named with
.Fl C ,
as the original compiler reads a file given with the
.Ql /C
switch; it is read before the sources.
Any other argument is a source file, whatever its name.
Each source is compiled separately, as a C compiler compiles its files; the
output for
.Pa NAME.imp
is
.Pa NAME.o ,
.Pa NAME.s
or
.Pa NAME.ll .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c
Compile to an object file
.Pa NAME.o
for each source and do not link.
The object is what a REL file is on TOPS-10: ELF, Mach-O or whatever the
system uses.
.It Fl S
Compile to the assembly of the target,
.Pa NAME.s .
.It Fl Sir
Compile to LLVM IR text,
.Pa NAME.ll .
.It Fl o Ar file
Name the output: the executable
.Pa a.out
by default.
It is an error to use
.Fl o
with
.Fl c ,
.Fl S
or
.Fl Sir
and more than one source.
.It Fl -target Ns = Ns Ar triple
Make output for another system (LLVM's names, the vendor may be left out:
.Ql aarch64-linux-gnu ,
.Ql riscv64-linux-musl ,
.Ql x86_64-apple-darwin ) .
Objects, assembly and IR are written for it.
The byte order of the target is taken from LLVM's data layout of it.
The architecture, operating system, environment and object format are checked
by LLVM's own triple parser, so the set follows the installed LLVM; the vendor
is free text.
A triple that fails the check is refused
.Pq Ql imp: unrecognized target ,
with status 1 and no output.
Linking for another system needs
.Fl -sysroot
and
.Fl -runtime ;
there is no runtime library for Windows or Cygwin.
.It Fl -sysroot Ns = Ns Ar dir
With
.Fl -target
and no
.Fl c :
the root of the target system, for its C library start files
.Pq Pa dir/usr/lib/crt1.o , No ... ,
or for macOS the SDK directory.
.It Fl -runtime Ns = Ns Ar file
With
.Fl -target
and no
.Fl c :
the runtime library built for the target.
.It Fl C Ar file
A file of syntax and declarations, read before the sources.
More than one may be named; they are read in the order given, and a source
compiled by itself (with several sources) reads them each time.
.It Fl s Ar letters
The switches of the original compiler, one letter each.
The listings and dumps go to the standard error.
.Ql A
the assembly listing (the chain of IR records),
.Ql L
the source listing,
.Ql G
the parser trace,
.Ql I
the directory of names,
.Ql N
the connectivity matrices,
.Ql P
the parse output and the semantics execution,
.Ql M
the code-tree matching,
.Ql S
the semantics array,
.Ql T
the code tree,
.Ql X
the syntax graph,
.Ql F
the free-storage map,
.Ql Z
statistics.
.It Fl I Ar image
Start from a compiler saved with the
.Ql /U
or
.Ql /V
switch of
.Fl i
instead of reading the syntax files.
.It Fl i
Read the original compiler's commands from the terminal:
.Ql OBJ,LIST_SOURCE/switches ,
one compilation for each line.
.Ql NAME_/U
saves the compiler as
.Pa NAME.img .
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl -help , Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev IMPSYNTAX
The syntax file, instead of
.Pa share/imp/syntax.imc .
.It Ev IMPFFI
The C interface file, instead of
.Pa share/imp/ffi.imc .
.It Ev IMPLIB
The runtime library linked into programs, instead of
.Pa lib/imp/libimp.a .
.It Ev IMPSYSROOT , Ev IMPRUNTIME
The values of
.Fl -sysroot
and
.Fl -runtime .
.It Ev SDKROOT
On macOS, the SDK to link with; otherwise the Command Line Tools' and then Xcode's.
.It Ev TMPDIR
The directory for the private directory that holds the object of a program to be linked,
when it is an absolute path naming a directory in which one can be made.
The default is
.Pa tmp/
of the source tree, and
.Pa /tmp
for an installed compiler.
No temporary file is made for
.Fl c ,
.Fl S ,
.Fl Sir
or
.Fl -run ,
and none for a link on Linux while an unnamed memory file can be used.
The directory and its files are deleted on exit and on
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGQUIT
and
.Dv SIGTERM .
.It Ev IMPNOMEMFD
If set and not
.Ql 0 ,
a link on Linux uses the private directory and not an unnamed memory file.
.It Ev IMPLDSHOW
If set, the arguments of the linker are printed to the standard error.
.It Ev PATH
.Xr ld.lld 1
is searched here, and then in the directory of the LLVM the compiler was built
with, when lld is not linked in.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/share/imp/syntax.imc
The syntax of IMP72.
.It Pa /usr/share/imp/ffi.imc
The extension that defines
.Sy CCALL ,
.Sy EXTERNAL ,
.Sy CALLBACK ,
.Sy FNPTR
and
.Sy STRUCT .
.It Pa /usr/lib/imp/libimp.a
The runtime library.
.It Pa /usr/include/imp.h
C declarations for code that shares data with IMP72 programs.
.It Pa a.out , Pa NAME.o , Pa NAME.s , Pa NAME.ll
Output.
.El
.Pp
The compiler finds these relative to its own place, so the prefix may be
other than
.Pa /usr .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
An error was reported in a source, a file could not be read or written, or
the link failed.
.It 2
The command line was wrong.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compile and run a program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp hello.imp && ./a.out
.Ed
.Pp
Compile two sources and link them with a C object:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp main.imp lib.imp util.o -o prog
.Ed
.Pp
An object for another system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp -c --target=aarch64-linux-gnu hello.imp
.Ed
.Pp
LLVM IR of a program, and the code tree of its compilation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp -Sir hello.imp
imp -s T -c hello.imp 2> tree.txt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr impstage0 1 ,
.Xr imp 7 ,
.Pa /usr/share/doc/imp/
.Sh AUTHORS
The original IMP72 is by
.An Walter Bilofsky ;
the IMP language by
.An Ned Irons .
